{
  "cells": [
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "# Inpainting, Outpainting & Image Editing Notebook\n\n> Hands-on Build It and Exercises."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## Build It"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "`code/main.py` implements a toy 1-D inpainting scheme on 5-dimensional data. We train a DDPM on 5-D mixture data where each sample is 5 floats from one of two clusters. At inference, we \"mask\" 2 of the 5 dimensions, inject the noisy-forward version of the unmasked three at each step, and regenerate only the masked dimensions."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "### Step 1: 5-D DDPM data"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```python"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "def sample_data(rng):"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    cluster = rng.choice([0, 1])"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    center = [-1.0] * 5 if cluster == 0 else [1.0] * 5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    return [c + rng.gauss(0, 0.2) for c in center], cluster"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "### Step 2: train denoiser over all 5 dims"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Standard DDPM. Net outputs 5-D noise prediction for 5-D noisy input."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "### Step 3: at inference, mask-aware reverse"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```python"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "def inpaint_step(x_t, mask, clean_image, alpha_bars, t, rng):"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    # replace unmasked dims with a freshly noised version of the clean source"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    a_bar = alpha_bars[t]"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    for i in range(len(x_t)):"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        if not mask[i]:"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "            x_t[i] = math.sqrt(a_bar) * clean_image[i] + math.sqrt(1 - a_bar) * rng.gauss(0, 1)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    # ...then run the normal reverse step on x_t"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "This is the naive approach and it works on toy 1-D data. Real image inpainting uses the 9-channel input because texture coherence matters more."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "### Step 4: outpainting"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Outpainting is inpainting with the mask inverted: mask the new (previously non-existent) canvas, fill the rest with the original. Identical training objective."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## Exercises"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "1. **Easy.** In `code/main.py`, vary the fraction of dimensions masked from 0.2 to 0.8. At what fraction does the inpaint quality (residual in masked dims) equal unconditional generation?\n2. **Medium.** Implement RePaint: at every 10th reverse step, jump back 5 steps (add noise) and re-denoise. Measure whether it reduces boundary residual at the mask edge.\n3. **Hard.** Use Hugging Face diffusers to compare: SD 1.5 Inpaint + ControlNet-Openpose vs Flux.1-Fill on 20 face-regeneration tasks. Score pose adherence and identity preservation separately."
      ]
    }
  ],
